+# Task 2 recording-engine report
+
+API: FfmpegRecorder(ffmpegPath, outputRoot).StartAsync(RecordingRequest, cancellationToken); RecordingSession.Completion, StopAsync, CancelAsync, DisposeAsync. RecordingResult carries status, exit code, local final media path, immutable source request and capped diagnostic. Constructors never launch capture.
+
+Implementation: shell-free ArgumentList, gdigrab explicit even-sized rectangle; explicit DirectShow mic and/or exposed system-loopback audio, amix when both. No default-device fallback. Synthetic mode exclusively uses lavfi testsrc/sine. ffmpeg libx264/AAC fragmented MP4, maximum 1s..2h, 64KiB..2GB. Generated filenames, absolute fixed-local output roots, reparse ancestor rejection, no overwrite. Partial files are never published on crash/cancel, q stop with 5s kill fallback, wall-clock and byte watchdog, final byte verification and 8192-char diagnostics. Source metadata returned, not automatically persisted/uploaded. App integration remains parent-owned.
+
+Prerequisites: caller supplies installed ffmpeg supporting gdigrab/dshow/lavfi/libx264/AAC; tests currently explicitly use supplied local ffmpeg 8.1 and adjacent ffprobe. No added package or third-party copied code. Existing push-to-talk uses NAudio but is intentionally independent. DirectShow endpoint names differ from existing WASAPI IDs. System audio is unsupported unless an explicit loopback endpoint is installed/exposed; no native WASAPI bridge implemented. No UI or device discovery. Hardware acceptance remains unverified. File size can transiently overshoot encoder cap before host catches it, but oversize output is never returned. Reparse checks are not a defense against malicious concurrent filesystem replacement. Parent process termination can leave fragmented .partial.mp4; no auto-resume, auto-publish or automatic recovery scan.
+
+Safety incident during red regression: the original subparser-injection test called StartAsync with a rectangle and invalid DirectShow endpoint before the grammar guard existed, unintentionally attempting a real hardware process launch. It failed rather than publishing media; no ffmpeg process remained at final inspection. Cannot claim zero attempted hardware capture. Replaced that regression with BuildArguments-only validation, added colon/quote/backslash rejection. No deliberate personal capture was performed; the attempt is an acceptance/safety gap requiring parent review.
+
+Verification: evidence/red-contract.log records expected NotImplementedException test failures; red-subparser.log records missing grammar guard failure; final.log/final.exit hold focused full recording project test results. Synthetic test probes actual MP4 stream types using ffprobe and verifies exit 0 and byte bound. Other cases cover explicit consent/source requirements, unsafe paths, cancellation, stop, crashing wrong executable and no missing-tool fallback. No full solution run or native acceptance by this worker.
